{"name":"Lichen Moss","hex":"#697056","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lichen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many symbiotic organisms, being associations of algae and fungi, often found as white or yellow-to-blue–green patches on rocks, old walls, etc.","Something which gradually spreads across something else, causing damage."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with lichen."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lichen"},{"term":"moss","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various small, green, seedless plants growing on the ground or on the surfaces of trees, stones, etc.; now specifically, a plant of the phylum Bryophyta (formerly division Musci).","A kind or species of such plants."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become covered with moss.","To cover (something) with moss."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moss"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lichen","blocks":[{"text":"A lichen is a hybrid colony of algae or cyanobacteria living symbiotically among filaments of multiple fungus species, along with bacteria embedded in the cortex, or \"skin\", in a mutualistic relationship. Lichens are the lifeform that first brought the term symbiosis into biological context."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Letharia_vulpina_JHollinger_crop.jpg/330px-Letharia_vulpina_JHollinger_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lichen","wikidata":"Q43142"},{"title":"Moss","blocks":[{"text":"Mosses are small, non-vascular flowerless plants in the taxonomic division Bryophyta sensu stricto. Bryophyta sensu lato may also refer to the parent group, bryophytes, which comprises liverworts, mosses, and hornworts. Mosses typically form dense green clumps or mats, often in damp or shady locations. The individual plants are usually composed of simple leaves that are generally only one cell thick, attached to a stem that may be branched or unbranched and has only a limited role in conducting water and nutrients. Although some species have conducting tissues, these are generally poorly developed and structurally different from similar tissue found in vascular plants. Mosses do not have seeds and after fertilisation develop sporophytes with unbranched stalks topped with single capsules containing spores. They are typically 0.2–10 cm (0.1–3.9 in) tall, though some species are much larger. Dawsonia superba, the tallest moss in the world, can grow to 60 cm (24 in) in height. There are approximately 12,000 species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Tionesta-ac-moss2.jpg/330px-Tionesta-ac-moss2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moss","wikidata":"Q25347"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg/500px-Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg/1280px-Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg/500px-Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_(7).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Caloplaca_marina.JPG/500px-Caloplaca_marina.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Caloplaca_marina.JPG/1280px-Caloplaca_marina.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Caloplaca_marina.JPG/500px-Caloplaca_marina.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACaloplaca_marina.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Caloplaca_thallincola.jpg/500px-Caloplaca_thallincola.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Caloplaca_thallincola.jpg/500px-Caloplaca_thallincola.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACaloplaca_thallincola.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Chrysothrix_chlorina.jpg/500px-Chrysothrix_chlorina.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Chrysothrix_chlorina.jpg/1280px-Chrysothrix_chlorina.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Chrysothrix_chlorina.jpg/500px-Chrysothrix_chlorina.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChrysothrix_chlorina.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Blistered_Jelly_Lichen_%28983550966%29.jpg/500px-Blistered_Jelly_Lichen_%28983550966%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Blistered_Jelly_Lichen_%28983550966%29.jpg/500px-Blistered_Jelly_Lichen_%28983550966%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikiped